00:17:35.940 --> 00:17:40.420
that sort of thing. And I thought I was zeroing in on what I considered

00:17:40.925 --> 00:17:41.965
a reasonable

00:17:42.125 --> 00:17:44.205
approach for the commission to

00:17:45.485 --> 00:17:46.605
take a look at.

00:17:48.365 --> 00:17:53.565
I sent that off to Mark Branch, and one of his comments was that he continues to

00:17:54.125 --> 00:17:55.165
strongly encourage

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:59.270
towns to make accessory dwelling units affordable housing,

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:03.910
which is a deed restricted

00:18:04.630 --> 00:18:05.670
thing for

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:09.110
10 as opposed to forty years, which a house would have.

00:18:13.825 --> 00:18:15.425
Being not

00:18:16.945 --> 00:18:21.745
the smartest guy in the world on how all the housing things work, I said, Okay, well,

00:18:22.305 --> 00:18:23.265
that's pretty easy.

00:18:23.745 --> 00:18:26.385
All that means is that we have to

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:30.020
rent restrict what can be charged.

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:32.820
And I think that's well below

00:18:32.980 --> 00:18:34.660
the threshold for what

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:41.860
you would charge for an accessory apartment anyhow. I think

00:18:41.995 --> 00:18:45.275
an accessory apartment would be affordable in terms of

00:18:45.595 --> 00:18:46.635
what you get there.

00:18:47.995 --> 00:18:48.635
So

00:18:49.275 --> 00:18:50.155
last month,

00:18:50.715 --> 00:18:55.275
when I was meeting with Eric and John, and I was discussing

00:18:55.900 --> 00:18:59.500
the plan, John's comment, his feedback was, well,

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:05.660
you might be discouraging people from building accessory apartments because

00:19:09.105 --> 00:19:10.305
There's paperwork

00:19:10.785 --> 00:19:14.465
that you have to do to document that the people going in

00:19:14.625 --> 00:19:17.025
are eligible for affordable housing.

00:19:19.585 --> 00:19:20.305
And that

00:19:20.705 --> 00:19:26.440
was a new thought to me. I never considered the fact that we would have to restrict

00:19:26.680 --> 00:19:28.280
who can rent

00:19:28.920 --> 00:19:31.240
the accessory dwelling unit

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:32.680
to

00:19:33.160 --> 00:19:33.800
those people.

00:19:35.405 --> 00:19:38.445
My experience in each of the accessory

00:19:38.445 --> 00:19:40.685
apartments that we've approved so far

00:19:41.325 --> 00:19:42.205
is that

00:19:43.165 --> 00:19:45.165
they were all for family members.

00:19:45.485 --> 00:19:46.125
Right.

00:19:46.845 --> 00:19:49.725
>> And we made arrangements so that when the family member,

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:56.130
no longer needed the apartment, it could be rented out. But I didn't recognize the

00:19:56.850 --> 00:19:59.490
burden on the property owner of

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:01.330
going through that.

00:20:02.050 --> 00:20:05.425
So, I went back to Mark Branch and said, Mark, what do you know about this? Is

00:20:07.745 --> 00:20:11.665
this the way it works? How does it work? How do you implement that plan?

00:20:13.345 --> 00:20:15.585
And he said, well, most towns have

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:17.910
a housing authority

00:20:18.230 --> 00:20:20.950
that tracks all that stuff. And

00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:32.615
we don't have that, so it would be something for Jim to do. And I'll ask John to chime in later on if he's got any experience with any other towns.

00:20:33.095 --> 00:20:34.215
But Mark Grants'

00:20:35.095 --> 00:20:39.815
bottom line comment was, are you going to do something about affordable housing or not?

00:20:40.375 --> 00:20:42.695
And if people want

00:20:43.015 --> 00:20:45.815
to make an accessory dwelling unit, accessory apartment,

00:20:47.150 --> 00:20:50.030
and the only way to do that is affordable,

00:20:50.590 --> 00:20:52.030
then they will probably

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:53.710
sign up to that.

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:57.150
I was

00:20:58.270 --> 00:21:00.510
thinking, you know, I looked to the future.

00:21:01.255 --> 00:21:02.855
At some point, I might

00:21:03.335 --> 00:21:07.255
want to go into an accessory apartment living at one of my kids' houses.

00:21:07.815 --> 00:21:08.375
And

00:21:08.615 --> 00:21:11.735
fortunately, I wouldn't meet the income requirement

00:21:12.855 --> 00:21:18.450
that's required to live there. And I said, maybe that's not the way we want to go here.

00:21:23.570 --> 00:21:26.210
But we could probably go either way in

00:21:26.690 --> 00:21:27.970
the

00:21:29.695 --> 00:21:32.255
It's not like we're building a ton of these things,

00:21:32.895 --> 00:21:33.375
but

00:21:34.175 --> 00:21:39.455
I was trying to get looking for some feedback from the commission members on whether they thought

00:21:39.855 --> 00:21:41.935
still requiring affordable

00:21:42.250 --> 00:21:45.050
requiring an accessory apartment to be affordable

00:21:45.530 --> 00:21:48.090
was the right way for us to go in

00:21:48.570 --> 00:21:51.130
an effort to try and make some headway on

00:21:51.770 --> 00:21:52.490
the

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:53.530
affordable housing.

00:21:54.585 --> 00:21:57.065
And John, before I open it up to the Commission,

00:21:57.465 --> 00:22:00.345
did you have anything to add? Do you have any experience on

00:22:00.585 --> 00:22:03.225
managing the admin in there?

00:22:03.785 --> 00:22:04.745
>> Yeah,

00:22:04.745 --> 00:22:10.585
a little bit. I mean, I've worked with a couple of towns. And again, I mean, Mark France is correct that

00:22:10.220 --> 00:22:12.700
there's usually an established administrative

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:14.060
procedure for this

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:18.380
housing authority, which goes through and does annual income verification.

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:21.500
But it a

00:22:22.220 --> 00:22:23.545
burden because

00:22:23.785 --> 00:22:28.745
in order for those accessory units to count towards the town's

00:22:29.465 --> 00:22:30.905
eight thirty gs

00:22:32.665 --> 00:22:33.385
list,

00:22:34.425 --> 00:22:36.905
its goals toward the 10% state threshold,

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:41.720
it does need to be accounted for on an annual basis.

00:22:43.720 --> 00:22:50.280
So you need to go through those income verification procedures and basically get the tax returns of the tenant,

00:22:51.945 --> 00:22:53.465
which is

00:22:54.265 --> 00:22:56.905
kind of a hassle. And most

00:22:57.305 --> 00:23:00.185
affordable housing landlords or

00:23:00.265 --> 00:23:03.545
housing authorities sort of have someone that's job is to do that.

00:23:05.740 --> 00:23:16.060
So, I mean, I think it's worth stepping back and just saying, what is the goal here? Is the goal to promote accessory apartments in general, or to try to leverage

00:23:16.140 --> 00:23:18.220
accessory apartments specifically

00:23:18.635 --> 00:23:22.875
as a way to make progress towards the state 10% goal.

00:23:23.195 --> 00:23:29.355
And if it is that latter thing, the towards the state goal, then we should think about

00:23:30.555 --> 00:23:32.395
assisting or providing some sort of

00:23:35.330 --> 00:23:36.130
incentive

00:23:36.530 --> 00:23:43.410
to those people that are gonna restrict their unit. Because, again, not only is there an administrative burden,

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:49.090
but restricting it to those people that are in the lower income

00:23:48.625 --> 00:23:49.425
bracket

00:23:50.145 --> 00:23:56.705
means that you will be restricted in what you can rent the thing for. So in theory, you're foregoing

00:23:57.585 --> 00:24:01.905
some rent, some percentage of market rent. So it's a little bit of

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:04.390
insult to injury. So if

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:08.550
you're trying to encourage people to do this, should at least try to make it easier for them to do so.

00:24:10.230 --> 00:24:17.625
>> Now it's my impression that whatever rent we would charge for the small unit there is well below the threshold of

00:24:18.185 --> 00:24:18.825
what

00:24:19.145 --> 00:24:22.345
somebody making 80% of the median income

00:24:23.065 --> 00:24:27.865
would be. I know when we did our study, we said somebody

00:24:27.865 --> 00:24:31.750
on that income could afford to buy a $300,000

00:24:31.750 --> 00:24:32.390
house.

00:24:32.950 --> 00:24:40.630
I figured the rent for any accessory dwelling unit would be well below what you would pay on a four bedroom home.

00:24:42.630 --> 00:24:46.075
So I don't know that you're giving up rent per se. And again,

00:24:46.315 --> 00:24:47.595
I was thinking that

00:24:50.475 --> 00:24:56.875
in most cases, it starts out being housing for a family member, either just starting out or just ending up.

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:05.290
But I would hate to preclude somebody from being able to take advantage of that kind of a deal because

00:25:05.450 --> 00:25:07.210
we can't

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:09.770
go through the admin that

00:25:10.090 --> 00:25:11.530
is required to

00:25:12.005 --> 00:25:15.845
provide that help to our family members if we chose to do so.

00:25:17.365 --> 00:25:18.965
I have a question.

00:25:19.205 --> 00:25:20.725
Yep, go ahead, Anne. If

00:25:23.205 --> 00:25:25.845
>> you go through and make this the affordable

00:25:25.845 --> 00:25:29.030
thing, does that mean that somebody can't build

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:32.070
an accessory apartment for their family member

00:25:32.710 --> 00:25:34.150
if it's

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:35.270
not affordable?

00:25:35.910 --> 00:25:41.110
I mean, like, we have an in law apartment on our house and we built it not because

00:25:42.035 --> 00:25:48.435
the in laws couldn't afford a bigger house. It's just that we wanted them close by for being able to help them

00:25:48.595 --> 00:25:52.515
to prevent going into an assisted living or a nursing home.

00:25:52.835 --> 00:25:57.395
So, that's why we built it not because it had to be affordable. Would that prohibit

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:01.050
building that kind of an in law accessory apartment?

00:26:02.010 --> 00:26:02.730
>> Certainly

00:26:03.610 --> 00:26:06.410
for the way we were proposing

00:26:06.890 --> 00:26:08.730
the accessory apartment thing,

00:26:09.050 --> 00:26:12.170
it would. It just says all accessory apartments will be

00:26:13.475 --> 00:26:16.995
affordable having ten year rent restrictions on it.

00:26:18.355 --> 00:26:19.795
Ann, your situation

00:26:20.835 --> 00:26:21.875
was exactly

00:26:22.835 --> 00:26:26.675
what I was looking at to say, that was a wonderful plan you had,

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:34.480
and would we do that? Now, sounds like John was going to disagree with me there, so I'll go back to him so he can

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:38.720
make us all smarter on how we might choose to get around that.

00:26:39.840 --> 00:26:42.000
>> No, sorry, Jed, you're correct.

00:26:43.335 --> 00:26:47.735
I had forgotten that that's what was on the table was mandating

00:26:47.735 --> 00:26:48.935
that if you do

00:26:49.735 --> 00:26:56.290
an accessory apartment, it needs to be restricted. So yeah, if that regulation goes in place or is in place, then

00:26:56.290 --> 00:27:01.650
Jed's answer is correct. I think what I meant to say is it certainly doesn't need to be that way.

00:27:03.890 --> 00:27:05.250
>> But it gets back to

00:27:05.810 --> 00:27:09.090
providing an incentive for a guy to put the rent restriction in there,

00:27:09.875 --> 00:27:20.195
and I wasn't sure how to do that. So anyhow, the bottom line at this point is that's why I'm not bringing the accessory apartment thing that I thought was very close back to you guys is

00:27:20.595 --> 00:27:22.595
it ran off the rails here

00:27:22.800 --> 00:27:24.160
within the last month,

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:30.240
and I haven't figured out how to get it back on the track yet. So no more discussion on that.

00:27:31.200 --> 00:27:31.600
>> Can

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:34.000
I make a possible suggestion?

00:27:34.575 --> 00:27:38.335
Sure. Go ahead, Eric. Is that you leave yourself an out

00:27:38.575 --> 00:27:41.855
and you say that as of right,

00:27:42.495 --> 00:27:47.055
you can build accessory dwelling units in all residential zones

00:27:47.990 --> 00:27:50.870
as, but they have to be affordable housing.

00:27:51.430 --> 00:27:53.350
By special permit,

00:27:54.150 --> 00:27:55.670
you can build one

00:27:55.910 --> 00:27:58.230
and have it not be affordable

00:27:58.230 --> 00:27:59.030
housing.

00:27:59.750 --> 00:28:03.805
That way you put the burden on them prove why

00:28:04.125 --> 00:28:06.925
they want to not do it as affordable housing.

00:28:07.645 --> 00:28:15.165
If they want to just do it, it's automatically affordable housing. If they want to argue with you about it, maybe you allow them to do it some other way.

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:19.290
>> Okay. I like that thought. And

00:28:19.690 --> 00:28:21.290
I'll take that back and

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:24.890
try to build that in for

00:28:25.290 --> 00:28:28.490
what we're looking at there. Because that's the kind of solution

00:28:28.490 --> 00:28:30.010
I would you know,

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:32.090
I'm looking for is that

00:28:32.865 --> 00:28:41.745
provide some incentive, and I don't know what that would be, or provide an out. And maybe the incentive is you don't have to do the paperwork of going through a special permit.

00:28:42.145 --> 00:28:44.625
That might be incentive enough for some people to

00:28:45.230 --> 00:28:46.510
make it affordable.

00:28:46.670 --> 00:28:48.670
What does John think about that?

00:28:55.710 --> 00:28:56.750
John, do want to comment?

00:28:57.655 --> 00:29:04.535
>> I'm sorry. What do I think of what I think of Eric's Of what Eric's suggestion was. Yeah. No. And again, I I think that's

00:29:05.495 --> 00:29:07.655
if if this commission is

00:29:10.770 --> 00:29:11.810
of the mindset

00:29:12.530 --> 00:29:16.530
that you want to do what you can to use accessory apartments

00:29:16.850 --> 00:29:18.770
as a mechanism for

00:29:20.930 --> 00:29:22.850
doing more to meet the state threshold,

00:29:23.485 --> 00:29:27.885
then I think Eric's suggestion is a good one. That, you know, the low hanging fruit,

00:29:27.965 --> 00:29:33.085
which is to say the as of right, you know, administrative zoning permit would be the restricted unit.

00:29:33.245 --> 00:29:34.285
And if you

00:29:34.765 --> 00:29:41.860
want to avoid that, then you would go through a more complicated process of a special permit. So that puts the incentives in the right place.

00:29:42.660 --> 00:29:46.900
And it doesn't outright prohibit someone like Anne from

00:29:47.140 --> 00:29:50.340
doing a unit, you know, an unrestricted unit for a family member.

00:29:51.060 --> 00:29:54.180
>> Right. Yeah. That sounds like a good idea to me too.

00:29:55.835 --> 00:29:57.275
>> Seems like it still

00:29:57.675 --> 00:29:58.555
discourages

00:29:58.555 --> 00:30:04.555
people from doing accessory dwelling units. I think it would be better if there was a way to incentivize

00:30:04.555 --> 00:30:06.635
people to do the affordable housing

00:30:06.875 --> 00:30:07.595
without

00:30:08.230 --> 00:30:12.070
putting a burden on them to go through a special permit process

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:13.910
if they don't want to do that.

00:30:14.790 --> 00:30:18.790
There John, do you have any experience with that where there's incentives that we can offer?

00:30:20.235 --> 00:30:20.955
>> Yes.

00:30:22.075 --> 00:30:24.715
So there's a couple of incentives

00:30:24.955 --> 00:30:27.115
that could be considered. I'm

00:30:27.115 --> 00:30:27.755
just

00:30:28.075 --> 00:30:32.075
giving examples here. I'm not necessarily advocating to spell something specifically for Andover, but

00:30:32.475 --> 00:30:34.315
some municipalities have considered

00:30:34.670 --> 00:30:35.470
providing

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:36.830
a tax abatement

00:30:36.830 --> 00:30:40.670
that essentially that the added value of the property

00:30:41.150 --> 00:30:48.190
or that accessory unit, you know, they add whatever increased value, you know, would be taxable could be abated

00:30:48.765 --> 00:30:51.245
or the town could offer a tax,

00:30:51.405 --> 00:30:56.845
you know, rebate or incentive of a couple $100 or something like that a year to do this.

00:30:57.165 --> 00:31:02.525
And if the town had administrative resources, and this is done primarily

00:31:02.525 --> 00:31:11.300
by larger towns or towns with a more robust administrative staff or a housing authority, is to basically offer to help people do that paperwork every year.

00:31:12.740 --> 00:31:14.740
So basically to eliminate those

00:31:15.060 --> 00:31:15.620
pain points.

00:31:17.845 --> 00:31:21.125
>> I think those are good ideas. I like the tax abatement

00:31:21.285 --> 00:31:24.885
issue. If somebody goes affordable housing, that's an incentive.

00:31:25.765 --> 00:31:26.485
I

00:31:27.365 --> 00:31:28.565
think it's

00:31:28.565 --> 00:31:28.965
less

00:31:30.130 --> 00:31:31.730
you know, the approach for it take

00:31:32.450 --> 00:31:36.370
taking is, I think, more punitive and discourages people from

00:31:36.770 --> 00:31:42.210
creating accessory dwelling units. I think if you do the tax abatement, it's more of an incentive approach,

00:31:42.370 --> 00:31:43.730
carrot versus a stick.

00:31:46.625 --> 00:31:48.145
>> I'm not sure, Steve, that,

00:31:48.945 --> 00:31:50.305
again, I think

00:31:50.385 --> 00:31:51.825
the rents on

00:31:52.385 --> 00:31:54.785
most accessory dwelling units

00:31:55.265 --> 00:31:56.785
are low enough

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:01.770
that they meet the threshold for affordable housing

00:32:02.970 --> 00:32:04.250
right off the bat.

00:32:04.570 --> 00:32:05.690
It's

00:32:05.690 --> 00:32:09.210
only a question of getting out of that admin of verifying

00:32:09.530 --> 00:32:11.290
that your tenant

00:32:11.455 --> 00:32:13.215
meets the threshold

00:32:13.215 --> 00:32:14.415
to go in there.

00:32:17.375 --> 00:32:19.215
And maybe I

00:32:19.375 --> 00:32:20.015
didn't

00:32:20.255 --> 00:32:25.520
dig a lot into the tax incentives because I don't think we've got the staff.

00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:28.240
And I thought that was clearly something that's not

00:32:29.440 --> 00:32:30.960
in Jim's thing to do.

00:32:36.615 --> 00:32:38.855
I don't know. I guess I go

00:32:38.855 --> 00:32:42.295
back to Eric. You know, Eric, does the town have

00:32:42.455 --> 00:32:52.775
the wherewithal to manage a tax incentive? I know we're trying to get the tax rules up, not down, but we do you know, I always like

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:53.480
carrots more than sticks.

00:32:55.480 --> 00:33:00.600
>> I think you would have a tough time convincing the Board of Selectmen that that was an appropriate

00:33:01.240 --> 00:33:01.880
venue.

00:33:03.080 --> 00:33:05.240
They've been pretty reticent

00:33:05.755 --> 00:33:08.235
to allow tax incentives,

00:33:08.555 --> 00:33:12.395
which is why we have no PA four ninety for farmers in Andover.

00:33:12.635 --> 00:33:13.275
Yep.

00:33:13.595 --> 00:33:14.235
So

00:33:14.795 --> 00:33:19.595
would they do it for affordable housing? You would have to make a really great case for it,

00:33:20.550 --> 00:33:25.270
And then you might get them to agree to it. But that would be a discussion between

00:33:25.590 --> 00:33:27.830
the zoning commission and

00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:28.630
the

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:33.030
Board of Select. That's certainly not anything I could influence

00:33:33.030 --> 00:33:33.430
on.

00:33:36.485 --> 00:33:38.725
>> Okay. Well, we'll consider

00:33:39.445 --> 00:33:40.485
that. My

00:33:42.085 --> 00:33:45.365
thought was pretty much in line with Eric that

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:49.940
there's a lot of tax incentives that we're not doing,

00:33:50.180 --> 00:33:52.740
and that's what's going to make the affordable housing thing

00:33:53.140 --> 00:33:57.460
a tough nut for us to crack as we get into it more. But

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:00.180
accessory dwelling units

00:34:00.735 --> 00:34:02.175
certainly will

00:34:02.175 --> 00:34:03.455
continue to

00:34:04.495 --> 00:34:06.495
add to the role of natural

00:34:06.495 --> 00:34:07.855
affordable housing.

00:34:08.655 --> 00:34:11.215
We just don't get any credit for it. And so

00:34:12.415 --> 00:34:14.735
is natural

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:21.150
affordable housing good? Yes, I think it is. Is it too bad that we don't get A credit? I wish there was a way we could do that too,

00:34:21.870 --> 00:34:25.950
because it's certainly easier to just go out and let the market dictate

00:34:26.030 --> 00:34:27.150
those smaller

00:34:28.375 --> 00:34:31.415
rents for smaller houses and apartments.

00:34:34.615 --> 00:34:35.895
Anything else on

00:34:36.775 --> 00:34:38.215
accessory apartments at this point?

